#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:2
msgid "Cypher Policies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:4
msgid ""
"|morpheus| allows administrators to set robust Cypher policies, which "
"determine global, role, and/or specific user access to configured Cypher "
"secret mount points. A number of considerations should be made when "
"deploying Cypher Access policies, including how user role permissions "
"will interact with the policy and how conflicts between overlapping "
"policies are resolved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:7
msgid "Role Permissions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:9
msgid ""
"User Role permissions (|AdmRol|) greatly affect Cypher access. Cypher "
"access Role permissions are set from the Features tab of the selected "
"Role under \"Tools: Cypher\". The Role permission should be set based on "
"the highest level of access to any one individual Cypher entry needed for"
" the specific Role. For example, if the Role needs no access to any "
"Cypher entries, set the feature permission to \"None\" and hide the "
"Cypher UI from the Role completely. Alternatively, if the Role needs to "
"use and decrypt even one Cypher entry, set the feature permission to "
"\"Full Decrypt\". The complete set of available permissions are below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:11
msgid "**NONE:** Cypher UI hidden"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:12
msgid "**READ:** Cypher UI present, entries can be listed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:13
msgid ""
"**USER:** Cypher UI present, user sees and can use their own entries, "
"user can create new entries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:14
msgid ""
"**FULL:** Cypher UI present, user sees and can use all entries, user can "
"create new entries, user cannot decrypt any entries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:15
msgid ""
"**FULL DECRYPT:** Full access to Cypher features including the ability to"
" decrypt secrets"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:18
msgid "Cypher Access Policies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:20
msgid ""
"Like other Policy types, Cypher Access Policies are created in |AdmPol|. "
"Click :guilabel:`+ ADD POLICY` to create new. Set the type to \"Cypher "
"Access\" and the relevant configuration options will be displayed. In "
"addition to the type, enter a name for the Policy in the top section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:22
msgid ".. image:: /images/administration/policies/polname.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:25
msgid ""
"In the next section, enter the key path to which the Policy will apply. "
"In addition to static entries that point to one specific Cypher entry, "
"this field supports pattern matching with regex. For example, enter "
"\".*\" to refer to all Cypher entries or \"secret/.*\" to refer to all "
"entries under the secret mount point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:27
msgid ""
"In addition to the path, set the privileges users in the Policy scope "
"should have on the indicated path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:29
msgid "**LIST:** See the entries on the indicated path listed in the Cypher UI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:30
msgid "**READ:** Decrypt the entries on the indicated path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:31
msgid "**WRITE:** Add new entries on the indicated path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:32
msgid "**UPDATE:** Edit Cypher entries on the indicated path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:33
msgid "**DELETE:** Delete entries on the indicated path"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:35
msgid ".. image:: /images/administration/policies/polconfig.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:38
msgid ""
"Finally, set the scope for the Policy. Cypher Access Policies support "
"Global, Role, and User scope. For example, you may want to block off sets"
" of Cypher entries for various departments within your organization. If "
"you have existing Roles in |morpheus| for each department, you can set up"
" Role-scoped Policies to ensure they can only list, use, and add Cypher "
"entries which are relevant to their own department."
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:40
msgid ".. image:: /images/administration/policies/polfilter.png"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:43
msgid ""
"When Cypher Access Policies conflict, the Policy with the longest path "
"string length (typically the most specific) takes precedence. For "
"example, a Policy giving LIST and READ access to \"secret/aws/.*\" would "
"be superseded by a Policy giving NO access to \"secret/aws/my-secret-"
"key\". In such a case, the user would see everything at the "
"\"secret/aws/.*\" path except the one indicated in the more specific "
"Policy. When Policies targeting the same path differ only in their scope,"
" the following scope precedence is applied: Role > User > Global. For "
"example, if a Role-scoped Policy targeting \".*\" grants LIST and READ "
"while a User-scoped Policy targeting the same path grants LIST, the user "
"would be granted the rights in the Role-scoped Policy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:46
msgid "Cloud Profiles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../administration/policies/cypherpolicies.rst:48
msgid ""
"Terraform Cloud Profiles are created on each Cloud detail page (|InfClo| "
"> Selected Cloud > Profiles Tab), encrypted in Cypher, and create a "
"Cypher entry that is visible both on the Profile tab of the Cloud detail "
"page and in Cypher. When added to a Cloud they create a Cypher entry at "
"path tfvars/profile/cloud/$cloudCode/variables. If a Cloud profile Cypher"
" entry is restricted by a Cypher Access policy, it will be (or will not "
"be) listable/readable/deletable as dictated by the Policy but still will "
"be viewable from the Cloud detail page if the user has sufficient "
"permissions. Restricting or granting access to Cloud profiles via Policy "
"does not affect access on the Cloud. Other Role permissions, such as "
"\"Admin: Profiles\", \"Infrastructure: Clouds\", and Cloud/Group access "
"must be used to restrict access via Cloud detail pages."
msgstr ""

